---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StateDial from "../components/StateDial.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Hona for lenders — free draw request access, granted by your borrower"
  description="Hona gives hard money lenders free read-only access to a borrower's project: budgets, receipts, progress photos, and draw requests you can approve from your phone."
>

  <section class="split">
    <div class="split__dark">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--brass">For lenders</p>
        <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2.25rem,1.5rem+3.4vw,3.9rem)">
          See the receipts behind the draw. Approve it from your phone.
        </h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Your borrower grants you access. Every request arrives with the costs,
          the photographs and the phase already attached. You approve, or you
          send it back with a note. There is nothing to buy and nothing for your
          team to install.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="split__light on-cream">
      <StateDial size="min(280px, 64vw)" plain={true} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-cream">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">What's different</p>
      <h2 style="max-width:24ch">Every other draw platform starts at the draw request.</h2>

      <div class="duo" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
        <p>
          Draw management software is sold to the lender and the borrower gets
          an account because you require one. It begins the moment a request is
          submitted, which means the numbers inside it are whatever the
          borrower typed that morning.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hona is bought by the contractor, for their own job costing. The draw
          request is assembled from receipts they were already capturing to run
          their business — so what you're approving is the work, documented as
          it happened, not a summary written to get paid.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-deep">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">What you get</p>
      <h2 style="max-width:20ch;margin-bottom:2.5rem">Everything you'd otherwise ask for by email.</h2>

      <div class="duo">
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:.75rem">On every project shared with you</h3>
          <p>
            Budget against actual, phase by phase and category by category. The
            receipts sitting behind each figure. Progress photographs as the
            work completes. Draw request status, live.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:.75rem">When a draw comes in</h3>
          <p>
            An email tells you a phase is ready. Open the project, look at the
            photos and the costs, and tap the mark to approve. Press and hold
            instead to send it back with a note about what you need first.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="rule" style="margin:3rem 0" />

      <p style="max-width:56ch">
        Approving in Hona records your approval. Funding happens through your
        own process, on your own rails, exactly as it does today. Hona does not
        move money and never will.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-billiard">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--pale">The terms</p>
      <h2 style="max-width:22ch">Free, permanently, and the contractor holds the keys.</h2>

      <ul class="ledger" style="max-width:52ch">
        <li><span>Cost to you</span><span>None</span></li>
        <li><span>Cost to your borrower</span><span>$29 / month</span></li>
        <li><span>Who grants access</span><span>The contractor</span></li>
        <li><span>Who can revoke it</span><span>The contractor</span></li>
        <li><span>Who keeps the history</span><span>The contractor</span></li>
      </ul>

      <p style="margin-top:2.5rem;max-width:56ch">
        That last line is deliberate. Competing portals are white-labelled under
        the lender's brand, so when a borrower changes lenders their project
        history disappears with the old relationship. In Hona the record belongs
        to the contractor and is shared with you — which is why a borrower will
        actually keep it current.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-ink">
    <div class="shell" style="text-align:center">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--brass">Next step</p>
      <h2 style="max-width:22ch;margin-inline:auto">Which borrower should see this first?</h2>
      <p style="margin:1.75rem auto 0;max-width:48ch">
        Tell us who to reach out to. We'll get them set up, and you'll receive
        an invitation to their project once they've added you. If you'd rather
        talk it through first, write to us directly.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row" style="justify-content:center">
        <a class="btn btn--brass" href="mailto:support@honafinancial.com?subject=Borrower%20introduction">
          Introduce a borrower
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
